def get_slide_data() -> dict:
    return {
        'title': 'What is a Shock Wave?',
        'content': '\n# What is a Shock Wave?\n\nA **shock wave** is a thin, nearly discontinuous wave front that forms when an object travels through a fluid (gas or liquid) **faster than the local speed of sound** ($M > 1$). It represents the fluid\'s inability to "warn" upstream regions of an incoming disturbance, resulting in an abrupt compression front.\n\n---\n\n## The Rankine–Hugoniot Jump Conditions\n\nAcross a **normal shock**, conservation of mass, momentum, and energy produce the jump relations:\n\n$$\\frac{\\rho_2}{\\rho_1} = \\frac{(\\gamma+1)M_1^2}{(\\gamma-1)M_1^2 + 2}$$\n\n$$\\frac{P_2}{P_1} = \\frac{2\\gamma M_1^2 - (\\gamma-1)}{\\gamma+1}$$\n\n$$\\frac{T_2}{T_1} = \\frac{P_2}{P_1} \\cdot \\frac{\\rho_1}{\\rho_2}$$\n\nWhere:\n- $M_1$ = upstream Mach number\n- $\\gamma$ = ratio of specific heats ($\\approx 1.4$ for air)\n- Subscripts $1$ (upstream) and $2$ (downstream)\n\n---\n\n## Bow Shock Geometry\n\nFor a **blunt body** like IRVE-3 at hypersonic speeds, the shock is **detached** — a curved bow shock stands off the nose. The stand-off distance $\\Delta$ scales with the inverse density ratio:\n\n$$\\frac{\\Delta}{R} \\approx \\frac{1}{\\frac{\\rho_2}{\\rho_1}}$$\n\nAt $M = 10$, the density ratio $\\rho_2/\\rho_1 \\approx 5.8$, creating a **tight shock layer** only $\\sim 0.17R$ ahead of the nose.\n\n---\n\n## Why It Matters for HIAD\n\n| Condition | Effect |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| Shock compression | $T_2 > 6000\\text{ K}$ stagnation temperature |\n| Downstream density jump | Thick, hot shock layer driving convective heating |\n| Curved shock geometry | Non-uniform heating distribution across torus stack |\n| Shock–boundary-layer interaction | Triggers scallop pocket heating between tori |\n\n<div style="margin-top: 1.5rem; display:flex; gap:1rem; justify-content:flex-end; flex-wrap:wrap;">\n    <a href="#" class="back-btn" style="margin-bottom:0; background:rgba(99,102,241,0.15); border-color:rgba(99,102,241,0.3); color:#a5b4fc;" onclick="loadTheoremSlide(0)">&larr; Back to Bow Shock Telemetry</a>\n    <a href="#" class="back-btn" style="margin-bottom:0; background:rgba(16,185,129,0.15); border-color:rgba(16,185,129,0.3); color:#6ee7b7;" onclick="loadTheoremSlide(3)">Kinetic Physics (Boltzmann &rarr; DSMC) &rarr;</a>\n</div>\n'
    }
